    "chunks": [
      "4 - He said: Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Bilal al-Muhallabi reported to me, he said: Ali ibn Abd Allah al-Isfahani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to me, from al-Husayn ibn Sufyan, from his father, from Abu Jahdham al-Azdi, from his father.",
      "He said: The reason for Abu Dharr's (may Allah have mercy on him) departure to al-Rabadha was that Mu'awiya wrote to Uthman: 'Abu Dharr sits in the mosque of Damascus, and the people gather around him, and he says things by which he criticizes the governors and speaks against them.' So Uthman wrote to Mu'awiya: 'Send Abu Dharr to me.'",
      "So he put him on a camel without padding, and he arrived having lost the flesh of his thighs. He was brought before Uthman, who said to him: 'May Allah not bring joy to one named Amr!' Abu Dharr said: 'My name is not Amr' - alluding to Amr ibn al-As. Then Uthman ordered that he be exiled to al-Rabadha and ordered that no one see him off. When Abu Dharr departed, the people went out with him to escort him until they reached Dayr Murran. Abu Dharr counseled and admonished them.",
      "Then he said: 'O people, I counsel you with what will benefit you: Testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and messenger. Then believe in the resurrection, paradise, and hellfire, and know with certain knowledge that Muhammad is the seal of the prophets, and that Ali is the Commander of the Faithful and the seal of the successors, and the imam of this era. Preserve my counsel; if you act upon it you will find its reward. I seek refuge in Allah for you from tribulation, for indeed Allah cares for His friend even if he is tested, and so that His decree may be fulfilled, for He has power over all things. Do not oppose His friends, do not antagonize them, and do not disobey them, for whoever opposes them is forsaken and whoever follows them is shown mercy.' Then the people bid him farewell and turned back.",
      "And he (Uthman) had ordered that no one from the people see him off. When the news reached the Commander of the Faithful Ali ibn Abi Talib (peace be upon him), he wept until his beard was soaked with his tears, then said: 'Is this how the companion of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and his family) is treated?! Indeed, we belong to Allah and to Him we shall return.' Then he rose, and with him were al-Hasan and al-Husayn (peace be upon them both), Abd Allah ibn al-Abbas, al-Fadl, Qutham, and Ubayd Allah, until they caught up with Abu Dharr and saw him off.",
      "And when Abu Dharr (may Allah have mercy on him) caught sight of them, he yearned toward them and wept for them, and said: 'May my father be sacrificed for faces that, when I see them, I remember the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and his family), and I am enveloped by the blessing of seeing them.' Then he raised his hands toward the sky and said: 'O Allah, indeed I love them, and even if I were cut limb from limb for their love, I would not cease from it, seeking Your countenance and the Abode of the Hereafter. So return, may Allah have mercy on you, and I ask Allah to be my best successor among you.' So the people bid him farewell and returned, weeping over his parting."
    ],
