    "seo_question": "Why was Abu Dharr al-Ghifari exiled to al-Rabadha and how did Imam Ali respond?",
    "summary": "This narration records the exile of Abu Dharr al-Ghifari (may Allah have mercy on him) to al-Rabadha by Caliph Uthman. Mu'awiya wrote to Uthman complaining that Abu Dharr sat in the Damascus mosque, attracting crowds while criticizing the governors and their policies of wealth accumulation. Uthman ordered Abu Dharr brought to him, and he was transported on a bare camel without padding, arriving with the flesh of his thighs worn away. After a terse exchange, Uthman exiled Abu Dharr to al-Rabadha and ordered that no one see him off. However, the people of Medina followed Abu Dharr to Dayr Murran, where he delivered a farewell sermon urging them to testify to the oneness of God, believe in the resurrection, paradise and hellfire, and recognize Muhammad as the seal of prophets and Ali as Commander of the Faithful and seal of the successors. When Imam Ali (peace be upon him) learned of Uthman's order, he wept until his beard was soaked with tears and went out with al-Hasan, al-Husayn, Ibn Abbas, al-Fadl, Qutham, and Ubayd Allah to bid Abu Dharr farewell. Abu Dharr was deeply moved, declaring his love for these faces that reminded him of the Prophet, and prayed to God that he would never abandon their love even if cut limb from limb. They parted in tears.",
